    <miscellaneous>
      <miscellaneous-field name="analysis-json">{"meta":{"id":"bach_bwv773_invention2","parent_item_id":"bach_bwv773_invention2","title":"Two-Part Invention No. 2 in C minor","composer":"Johann Sebastian Bach","source_file":"files/scores/bach_bwv773_invention2.mid","quarter_range":[0,108.0],"bar_quarters":4.0,"time_signature":"4/4","key":"c","tempo":64.0,"midi_play_url":"files/scores/bach_bwv773_invention2.mid","source_licence":"PD","source_page":"https://www.mutopiaproject.org/cgibin/piece-info.cgi?id=58","notation_notes":"27 bars of 4/4 from quarter 0, no pickup (the subject starts after an eighth rest), no repeats. Score reconstructed from the Mutopia MIDI (Bach-Gesellschaft source, PD); notes checked against the Mutopia .ly. The .ly prall/downprall trill signs are not in the MIDI; bars 2, 4, 12, 14 and 24 carry only a written-out C–D (or G–A) turn. MIDI-derived score, not an Urtext. Two-voice counterpoint implies harmony; most half-bars carry neighbour and passing 16ths, so many spans are left as ? with the implied function in the reason.","suggested_arrangement_tempo":64,"sustain_events":[]},"draft":false,"reviewed_by":"claude-opus-5-5 (MusicBot) 2026-10-11","summary":"A two-voice canon in two halves. Bars 1–11: the RH subject is followed two bars later by the LH an octave below, moving from C minor to E♭ major. Bars 11–20: the same canon transposed to G minor with the voices exchanged (LH leads), moving to B♭. A two-bar sequence (21–22) leads back to C minor, where the subject returns over its own counter-line (23) and is imitated in the bass (25) before a PAC on a bare C octave.","sections":[{"name":"Canon I (c → E♭)","start_bar":1,"end_bar":10,"mood":["brooding","restless"],"description":"RH subject, LH canon at the lower octave from bar 3; ii–V7–I cadences settle in E♭."},{"name":"Canon II, voices exchanged (g → B♭)","start_bar":11,"end_bar":20,"mood":["searching","darker"],"description":"Bars 1–10 a fifth lower with LH leading; arrives in B♭ at bar 17."},{"name":"Sequence back to C minor","start_bar":21,"end_bar":22,"mood":["tense","driving"],"description":"D7 and a stepped sequence lead to G7/B; LH scale falls to the dominant."},{"name":"Tonic return and close","start_bar":23,"end_bar":27,"mood":["resolute","sombre"],"description":"Subject in RH, then in LH two octaves lower; vii°7–V–i and a bare final octave."}],"mood":[{"start_bar":1,"end_bar":10,"labels":["brooding","restless"]},{"start_bar":11,"end_bar":20,"labels":["searching","darker"]},{"start_bar":21,"end_bar":22,"labels":["tense","driving"]},{"start_bar":23,"end_bar":27,"labels":["resolute","sombre"]}],"tricks":["Bars 1–11: a strict canon at the octave with a two-bar delay. One line is enough material for the whole first half.","Bar 11: the canon is restarted with the voices exchanged and transposed to G minor (invertible counterpoint); bars 11–20 mirror bars 1–10.","Bars 4–10 and 14–20: repeated ii–V7–I cells (Fm–B♭7–E♭, Cm–F7–B♭) make each relative-major key area sound settled.","Bars 22 and 24: long stepwise bass descents drive into the tonic arrivals.","Bar 27: ends on a bare C octave, leaving out the minor third."],"loop_candidates":[{"start_bar":1,"end_bar":2,"why":"Solo subject; the bar-2 D trill (V) leads back to the bar-1 C. The single line leaves room for added harmony.","quarter_range":[0,8],"requires_audition":true},{"start_bar":5,"end_bar":8,"why":"Two ii–V7–I cycles in E♭; bar 8 ends on B♭7/D, which resolves to the E♭ at bar 5.","quarter_range":[16,32],"requires_audition":true}],"hook_candidates":[{"start_bar":1,"end_bar":2,"why":"The subject: rising C–B–C–D–E♭, the drop to G, then the falling turn."},{"start_bar":22,"end_bar":23,"why":"Falling LH scale into the tonic return of the subject."}],"kpop_uses":[{"where":"verse","why":"Bars 1–2 loop as a solo minor-key riff under a beat; add a bass on C–F–G."},{"where":"pre-chorus","why":"Bars 21–22: sequence and falling bass build into the downbeat of bar 23."},{"where":"chorus","why":"Bars 23–26: subject in both hands with a full vii°7–V–i close. No key lift in the source."},{"where":"bridge","why":"Bars 11–14: the same riff in G minor in the bass voice for a darker contrast."}],"review_flags":[{"bar":5,"reason":"E♭ read as an established modulation (bars 5–11). It could also be read as a long tonicization of III within C minor."},{"bar":11,"reason":"The arrival on beat 1 is E♭/G; G minor could be read from beat 1 (iii in E♭ = i in g). LH leader alters the subject head (E♭–F instead of G–F♯); RH follower from bar 13 is exact."},{"bar":21,"reason":"Bar 21 kept in B♭ with D7 as V7/vi; it could equally be read in g or c. Beat 4 F5 over A–D–C is left unlabelled."},{"bar":26,"reason":"Beats 3–4 contain no B♮; the cadence is labelled PAC from the bass G2–C2 and the D–C trill, not from a sounding V triad."},{"bar":27,"reason":"Final chord is a bare octave; marked ? because no third sounds. The tonic is implied."},{"bar":1,"reason":"Many spans are ? because two-voice 16th counterpoint leaves implied chords under the 70% coverage rule; each reason names the implied function."}],"schema_version":1,"bars":[{"bar":1,"start_quarter":0.0,"duration_quarters":4.0,"key":"c","chords":[{"beat":1.0,"symbol":"?","rn":"?","function":"unresolved; implied i","reason":"Solo line C–B–C–D–E♭–G; B and D neighbours dilute the implied tonic"},{"beat":3.0,"symbol":"Fm7","rn":"iv7","function":"predominant outlined by the falling line A♭–F–E♭–C"}],"events":[{"type":"form","technique":"theme_1","text":"Canon I: RH states the two-bar subject alone (r8 C–B–C–D–E♭–G–A♭–B♭–A♭–F–F–E♭–D–C)"},{"type":"story","technique":"start","text":"start: a single anxious line in C minor"},{"type":"hook","technique":"melodic_cell","text":"Subject head C–B–C–D–E♭ then the drop to G and the turn A♭–B♭–A♭–F","beat":1.5}]},{"bar":2,"start_quarter":4.0,"duration_quarters":4.0,"key":"c","chords":[{"beat":1.0,"symbol":"?","rn":"?","function":"unresolved; implied vii°7–i","reason":"Solo line; B–A♭–G–F then E♭–D–C–B out